Introduction
An increase in human activities during the last few centuries (19th, 20th and 21st) are having negative impacts on the natural ecosystems, and humankind lifestyles and health (Rodrigo-Comino et al., 2020; Trenberth, 2018; Weber, 2003). One of the most important human activities is the increase in land consumption (Panagos and Katsoyiannis, 2019; Salvati and Carlucci, 2016) and the lack of sustainable and efficient land management plans (Cohen-Shacham et al., 2016; De Groot et al., 2010; De Sainte Marie, 2014). As a consequence, humankind will have to face adverse land degradation processes and difficult challenges related to their impacts such as global warming (Dai, 2011; IPCC, 2018), water pollution (Kumar et al., 2019), loss of soil fertility and productivity (Pieri, 1992; Wu et al., 2011), diseases such as the COVID-19 (Reuschke and Felstead, 2020) and light pollution (Longcore and Rich, 2004). Light is a key resource for the activation of some vital processes such as photosynthesis, the partition of daily and night activities or help to recover and repair organisms, and conservation of light environments. Also, it informs the circadian clocks and photoperiodism, helping visual perception and spatial orientation (Gaston et al., 2013). Artificial lights used to illuminate streets, homes and buildings emit light to the lower atmosphere that remains there due to the high concentration of aerosol particles, and continuous emission can be dangerous (Ściężor and Czaplicka, 2020). Bugnot et al. (2019) stated that light pollution is normally assessed at local scales, but there is a mismatch between ‘the spatial extent of the impacts and that of the pressures driving those impacts’. Light pollution is defined as a ‘multi-facet’ term (Luginbuhl et al., 2010), and to date, there is no a clear understanding of its impacts on human health (Cho et al., 2015; Rybnikova et al., 2016) and the natural ecosystems (Grubisic et al., 2018b; Guetté et al., 2018). The concentration of elevated amounts of electromagnetic energy during night-time from the Earth’s surface light sources in specific territories are having negative impacts on astronomical observations, and also on living organisms, including humans (Kocifaj, 2016). Kyba et al. (2017) estimated that artificial light transferred to the atmosphere could reach 2.2% per year, with considerable differences among countries and hemispheres, but will remain stable or decrease in a few areas. Their results are not consistent with other assessments made to estimate energy reductions at a global scale but highlighted the increasing trend in light pollution. Following Kyba’s (2018) recommendations, people (inhabitants, stakeholders and policymakers) awareness would be vital to modify lighting technologies and emissions. Macgregor et al. (2017) stated that humankind requires ‘suitable lighting technologies and concepts which are ecologically, socially and economically sustainable’. They insist that managing darkness is an essential part of future conservation and lighting management plans of each modern society over the world is imperative. However, without a global consensus, unpredictable environmental and sociological outcomes could appear. This cannot be reached without a complete understanding of this multifaceted problem. Considering the worldwide population growth, the elevated concentration of inhabitants in urban areas and the corresponding infrastructures and services (industries, roads, airports, railways, etc.), light pollution is an environmental issue showing an exponentially increasing trend that is difficult to control. As a result, the scientific community have been paying attention to this issue during the last two decades. The scientific literature on light pollution is diverse, focussing on different impacts of light pollution (animals, human well-being, etc.), using distinct methods or approaches (e.g. remote sensing techniques, surveys, laboratory analysis, modelling tools, etc.) and considering different spatiotemporal scales (e.g. experimental plots, urban or rural areas, regions, country). This shows the great advances conducted to assess, reduce or avoid this environmental problem. However, we consider that the reader (scientists, policymakers or stakeholders) could lose the general perspective of the problem if their expectations to get an overall idea of the problem is not satisfied.


