Conducting a simple search using the ISI Web of Science to find literature up to 2019 that contain the terms ‘light pollution’, >8000 articles were found (Figure 1). Obviously, not all of the articles aim to investigate light pollution. Therefore, the main goal of this review paper was to assess the whole available literature related to the term ‘light pollution’, paying attention to the (i) type of publications, year and journal; (ii) impacts on specific elements; (iii) location and (iv) methods used. We hypothesise that the number of specific papers aimed to investigate light pollution is high and more diverse (coming from different disciplines, methods, places and different scales). Besides, we believe that this review will be able to group the total impacts of light pollution on the Earth, showing some contradictory results, summarising solutions and offering future research directions.
Materials and methods
To achieve the above-mentioned goal, we searched the term ‘light pollution’ in the ISI Web of Science (https://apps.webofknowledge.com/WOS_GeneralSearch_input.do?product=WOS&search_mode=
