GeneralSearch&SID=F5ZJLB669fH5Bx5kkcR&preferencesSaved=) from the beginning of the database (2003) to 2019. A total of 8051 publications were obtained on October 3, 2020. It needs to be pointed out the authors of this review paper collectively can work in English, German, Spanish and French languages. However, we restricted our search on publications written in English only for the general scientific community, considering English as a vehicular language (Clouet, 2017; Gordin, 2015). During the literature selection, all the abstracts were assessed to select the only ones containing the information about light pollution in the introduction, methods, results or conclusions. Books were not included and only one PhD thesis, indexed in this database, was taken into account. In the end, 621 publications were used. Therefore, there may be valuable information published in other literature finders (e.g. Google Scholar), other different publication formats or distinct languages that were not considered. The information was organised and classified in an Excel datasheet under the following criteria: (i) journals; (ii) year of publication; (iii) type of format (considering different editorial and journal formats); (iv) impacts on specific Earth’s species or elements (animals, humans, light pollution in general and urban or rural areas); (v) location (place and country) and (vi) methods used. Some issues were found during the literature search (June 2019–October 2020) of the systematic review considering Haddaway et al. (2020). Regarding the division of the impacts on specific elements, we paid attention to the main topic highlighted in the title, abstract and the main conclusions. In fact, some of the articles could be classified into different groups due to the multidisciplinary nature of the topic. The study area or the country, where the investigation was conducted were difficult to find in some manuscripts. Therefore, if the specific study area or laboratory was not mentioned in the text, we tagged that manuscript as ‘non-classified’. For some of them, we took the place of the laboratory where the assessment or models were applied. In the case of the remote sensing applications, the location of the investigation was used as the study area. The complete list with all the titles used in this review will be posted on the website of the Smart Light-HUB (http:// smartlighthub.eu/), which is an international project (INTERREG) related to the co-creation, diffusion and transfer of knowledge on light pollution, that funded this study. Specific details will be shared upon request.
Results and discussion
Publication trends and journals
A total of 621 publications were selected in this review. As above-mentioned, these articles mainly investigate light pollution and discussed its impacts on the specific sphere. Figure 2 indicates that only 10 articles were published up to 2005, and 7 were recorded in 2006. However, a linear increase was registered (R2 = 0.87) from 2007 to 2019, reaching >100 papers in 2019. These results show the increasing importance of this topic for the scientific community just like other important environmental issues such as urban climate change (e.g. Sharifi, 2021), urban sprawl, food security and agricultural systems in developing countries (e.g. Abu Hatab et al., 2019), strategic environment assessments (e.g. Caschili et al., 2014), soil erosion (e.g. Nearing et al., 2017; Rodrigo-Comino, 2018) or emission of pesticides to the air (e.g. Gil and Sinfort, 2005). Journals with five or more publications related to light pollution are presented in Figure 3. It is worthy to highlight the diversity of the journals where the 276 papers were published. There are at least 190 scientific journals with at least one article on light pollution. The journals with the highest number of papers published on this topic in decreasing order are (i) Journal of Quantitative Spectroscopy and Radiative Transfer (32); (ii) Scientific Reports (18); (iii) Environmental Pollution (16); (iv) Journal of Experimental Zoology – A (16) and (v) Lighting Research & Technology (15).


