The scope of the journals with the most articles (Figure 3) aim at astronomy (e.g. Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society or Publications of the Astronomical Society of the Pacific), miscellanea (e.g. Scientific Reports, Plos One or Science of the Total Environment), remote sensing applications and developments (e.g. Remote sensing or Remote sensing of the Environment), ecology and biology (e.g. Global Change Biology, Proceedings of the Royal Society B: Biological Sciences or Journal of Applied Ecology) and engineering (e.g. Lighting Research & Technology, Applied Optics or Sensors). The ensuing sections demonstrate whether the increase in the number of publications and diversity of journals is linked to an ‘increase of actionable knowledge’ (Bornmann and Mutz, 2015). This is the key to develop efficient solutions and sustainable management plans.
Global distribution of publications
Figure 4 is depicted the number of publications on light pollution per country between 2003 and 2019, and Table 1 notes the year of publication per country. It is important to mention that it was not possible to classify 134 articles because there was no information on the study area, laboratory or any specific place. Here, we also included review papers, editorials, letters to editors, etc. Twenty-three articles considered two or more study areas from different countries. A total of 57 different countries were found in studies assessing light pollution from different points of view. Research groups from Chile, Italy and the USA published the first pioneering articles indexed in ISI Web of Knowledge in 2003. The country with the highest number of publications is the USA (91), which experienced an increase in research from 9 in 2013 to 18 articles per year in 2019. Several studies were conducted in Columbus under laboratory conditions (e.g. Cissé et al., 2017; Sullivan et al., 2019) or in big cities such as New York, Houston, Los Angeles or Las Vegas (e.g. Cabrera-Cruz et al., 2019; Duriscoe et al., 2018). Germany (40) followed the USA with an average of ≈4 articles per year of publications since 2013, with peaks in 2014 (6) and 2018 (9). There are several studies conducted in Berlin (e.g. Jechow et al., 2019; Kyba et al., 2011), Munich (e.g. Caorsi et al., 2019) or Potsdam (e.g. Puschnig et al., 2014) that assessed the problems related to urban areas. Also, it is relevant to remark on the research plans performed at the Westhavelland Nature Park (e.g. Grubisic et al., 2018a; Manfrin et al., 2018). Australia also registered an elevated number of publications with more than five papers per year from 2015 in Melbourne (e.g. Willmott et al., 2019), Sidney (e.g. Haddock et al., 2019) and Brisbane (e.g. Levin et al., 2014), mostly focussed
