Methodologies and tools
To facilitate the understanding of the wide spectrum of methods used to assess light pollution, we first divided them into formats published by the different journals and editorials (Figure 5) and, then, on the methods applied (Figure 6). A total of 34 different formats were found in all the surveyed journals with the format-method classification. About 80% of the articles were published under ‘research article’ format, followed by 9.9% under review format and the remainder falling under the other formats (e.g. editorials, brief communication, communication, short communication or scientific note). The first group under methods is composed of articles related to sampling and surveys (35.1%). This group included techniques such as nested sampling design using light traps that catch insects or birds during different nights (Merckx and Van Dyck, 2019). Also, night-time transects and overhead flight activity surveys were commonly used for 1-day fieldwork campaigns (Macgregor et al., 2017). Non-invasive techniques through laboratory observations (Newman et al., 2015), questionnaires (Mascovich et al., 2018) and in combination with other methods such as images obtained from Google Earth (Jin et al., 2017) were also used. Despite that they are the most commonly used methods (high accuracy supposed after using in situ measurements), several articles remark the difficulties related to (i) unforeseen weather conditions; (ii) obstacles in the terrain (Ciach and Fröhlich, 2019) and (iii) the elevated time exposure and costs required to gather enough samples meaningful results (Russo et al., 2019). The second group is characterised by remote sensing techniques (19.5%). Here, we included methods related to differential photometry with calibrated digital cameras (Jechow, 2019). Also, night-time images were taken with DSLR cameras from the International Space Station (ISS) (Sánchez de Miguel et al., 2019), Luojia 1-01 or the Suomi National Polar-Orbiting Partnership Visible Infrare