al., 2018; Zhao et al., 2019). It is important to remark some relevant concepts extracted from these articles such as the ‘negative luminescence’ (Gandy, 2017), ‘biodiversity-sensitive cities and towns’ (Ikin et al., 2015), ‘light pollution and passive pleasures, and the instrumental value of beauty’ (Gallaway, 2010). Also, the term ‘polarised light pollution’ is used as a new kind of ‘ecological photopollution’ (Horváth et al., 2009) or the ‘ecological light pollution’ (Longcore and Rich, 2004), which is referred to the alteration of natural light regimes in terrestrial and aquatic ecosystems. Finally, it is worthy to note the elevated number of review papers related to human psychiatry (e.g. Hoare et al., 2019) and wellbeing, considering topics about ageing (e.g. Parrado et al., 2019), sleep time (e.g. Mantua et al., 2019), diabetes (e.g. Afroz-Hossain et al., 2019), etc. Laboratory (15.8%), modelling (7.7%) and monitoring (3.6%) techniques were used in combination with the above-mentioned methods. Therefore, only fewer papers have been published using these methods alone.
Impacts on different specific issues
The number of publications on light pollution considering the different impacts on specific issues, living organisms and processes are summarised in Table 2.
Natural ecosystems, animals and aquatic ecosystems. A total of 269 studies have been published on animals and light pollution. Over the last decade, there has been an explosion of investigations related to the biological impacts of light pollution such as on migration, sleep time or health (Gaston et al., 2015; Lennox et al., 2016). Figure 7 summarises the most studied species of birds (69), insects (57) and bats (44). According to several studies conducted about birds, sleep time and behaviour did not differ under dark conditions but light pollution extremely modifies this trend depending on intensities. For example, Sun et al. (2017a) comparing blue and great tits demonstrated more pronounced effects and more aspects of sleep were affected for the latter. They fell asleep later, woke up and exited the nest


