halide and LED lights on the streets. They demonstrated that white LEDs do not attract more insects than the others, highlighting the necessity to use these lights to reduce disturbances across multiple insect taxa. Recently, Larsson et al. (2020) demonstrated similar behaviour as the above-mentioned for birds, but for Trichoptera, whose attraction to light varied among species, females are more influenced than males. These authors registered an atypical flight activity when the lamps were turned on at night. Yuen and Bonebrake (2017) observed that artificial lights at night could reduce prey interception for spiders too, which makes simple predictions of ecological light pollution’s disruption of species interactions difficult. Both aerial and terrestrial insects are being affected. In their recent review papers, Habel et al. (2019) and Fox (2013) estimated a clear reduction of terrestrial insects among others, adding light pollution to other supplementary and adversely synergistic factors such as climate change, urbanisation and associated processes (e.g. traffic volume or air/water pollution). Bats are nocturnal animals, and, thus, they are one of the most vulnerable species affected by light pollution (e.g. Lacoeuilhe et al., 2014; Musila et al., 2019; Straka et al., 2019). Rydell et al. (2017) highlighted that artificial lights of churches, and other historical buildings for aesthetic purposes, are some of the major problematic places for bats. However, this environmental issue is generally not considered in environmental impact assessments by public agencies. They proposed


