the installation of flood-lights on these structures. To reduce the impacts of light pollution on bats behaviour and foraging, Stone et al. (2015) proposed the avoidance of outdated, poorly installed and/or maintained lights, modification of the duration and timing of lighting regimes, its intensity and the types. To date, the impacts of light pollution on bats and hibernation, fitness costs or longterm implications have not been addressed. Azam et al. (2018) suggested separating streetlights from ecological corridors (≈50 m) without vertical light trespass. Also, it is relevant to highlight the number of articles aimed to assess animals in aquatic environments such as fishes (23), diverse aquatic ecosystems (21), turtles (19) or toads and frogs (9). Depledge et al. (2010) remark in their editorial that surprisingly light is one particular pollutant permitted to flood into the aquatic ecosystems and it is not foreseen to be addressed in the near future. Increasing illumination of the coastal zones, reefs, rivers, estuaries, deltas, gulfs and nearshore areas is threatening aquatic animals all over the world (e.g. McMahon et al., 2017; Nobes et al., 2008; Tamir et al., 2017; Zapata et al., 2019), even across deep profiles (Bolton et al., 2017). Davies et al. (2016) stated that it is imperative to improve our understanding of the ecological impacts of light pollution on marine ecosystems and they proposed the allocation of ‘Marine Dark Sky Park’. Other studies assessing the impacts of light pollution focussed on lemurs, roes, mice, squirrels, hamsters, voles, lizards, geckos, salamanders, some primates or wallabies. In this group, it is relevant to assess the results to evaluate potential human responses and the elevated percentage of studies conducted under laboratory conditions.
Human health and sociological aspects. In their review paper, Davies and Smyth (2018) indicated that human health and human-environment interrelationships must be considered as key questions for light pollution research in the 21st century, among other topics such as diversity and sensibility of biological responses, solutions or the expansion of LED lights. Dominoni et al. (2016) published an opinion piece that affirmed the scarcity of information on light pollution and the underlying physiological mechanisms affecting metabolic syndromes, cancer and reduced immunity of humans. We found a total of 77 articles published that shed new insights on this topic. Without a doubt, the most well-known negative impacts of artificial lights are on circadian rhythms and sleep disorders (Cho et al., 2013; Touitou, 2015). However, some studies verified that practical effect sizes could be smaller than expected and more associated with idiosyncratic issues (Patel, 2019). Twelve papers aimed to investigate the impacts of light pollution on mental disorders such as depression, Alzheimer and Parkinson (e.g. Kim et al., 2018). Moreover, some authors observed that elevated exposure under artificial lights at night could be linked to the prevalence of depressive symptoms and suicidal behaviour. We found two types of studies confirming these effects depending on the method. The first, type of studies used rodents while the second used representative human samples (Bedrosian and Nelson, 2013). For example, Bedrosian et al. (2011) and Fonken and Nelson (2013) found that light pollution can contribute to changes in the mood of hamsters and mice, which could be vital for evaluating and developing solutions for depressive disorders. On the other hand, Min and Min (2018) developed cross-sectional research using a representative sample of Korean inhabitants and found that light pollution could be considered as an ‘environmental contributor’ to mental health problems. Ten articles are related to the occurrence of cancers. Giraudeau et al. (2018) highlighted that humans can be defined as an ‘oncogenic species’ because they modify the environmental conditions causing cancer in other wild populations, or even, in other humans. In the study conducted by Xu and Gao (2017), an increase in artificial lights has a positive correlation with health index for inhabitants older than 60 ages (for 2011), cancer rate (for 2009) and surface temperature in Beijing (for 2012).


382 The Anthropocene Review 10(2)
We observed that breast cancer is the main studied type of cancer studied. It is related to the nocturnal suppression of melatonin production in the pineal gland under a high artificial light exposure, which some authors hypothesise that could be reduced by avoiding light at night (KeshetSitton et al., 2016). The cancer risks are associated with both women men, and increasing the duration of night work is not the key factor (Parent et al., 2012). It is worth highlighting other specific investigations focussed on the possible effects related to atherosclerosis (Obayashi et al., 2019), body mass (Fonken et al., 2010), ocular issues such as fatigue (Park, 2018), retina degeneration (Benedetto and Contin, 2019), skin deterioration (Parrado et al., 2019) or even diabetes (Afroz-Hossain et al., 2019). Despite the elevated number of studies confirming the negative impacts on human health, there is ongoing debate concerning the necessity to reduce or eliminate lights but lights in urban help to reduce crime, improves transportation safety and minimises concealment, aesthetic pleasing and helps with astronomical observations (e.g. Chepesiuk, 2009; Jones, 2018; Kim et al., 2015). Nevertheless, Schulte-Römer et al. (2019) prefer distinguishing between ‘lighting professionals’ having lighting design or/and industry background, and ‘light pollution experts’ who specialised in astronomy and environmental science issues. Fortunately, these authors agree on the issue definition and problem-solving advances facing light pollution, but they disagree on some other questions such as potential difficulties to mitigate light pollution impacts and related governance challenges. Also, some other studies show how ordinary inhabitants agree with some questions related to artificial lights. For example, Haans and de Kort (2012) surveying stationery and walking participants, confirmed that urban inhabitants prefer to illuminate their close surroundings instead of the roads. In Finland, 2053 respondents considered light pollution as an issue for outdoor recreation and relaxation and should be considered by policymakers and land planners (Lyytimäki and Rinne, 2013). These ambiguities manifest the necessity to consider light pollution with hindsight to their moral implications and ethical views (Stone, 2017).
Light pollution in general terms. We observe that 125 articles aimed to investigate general or specific aspects related to skyglow, night-time and night sky brightness. Urban measurements differed from those in suburban territories, which could demonstrate a mixed-effect model with different results at a regional scale (Fu et al., 2018; Kuechly et al., 2012; Ściężor et al., 2012). This makes astronomical observations difficult as the lighting sources are far away from the Earth (Barducci et al., 2003; Plauchu-Frayn et al., 2017). Therefore, light pollution indexes (e.g. Saraiji and Oommen, 2012; Votsi et al., 2017) and theoretical/mathematical models (e.g. Kocifaj, 2008, 2011) are useful tools considering the size, shape, spatial distribution, radiative pattern and spectral characteristics from the light sources, atmospheric conditions and orography. These new advances are helping to develop new maps (e.g. as the world atlas of artificial sky luminance) and quantifications for the most affected areas, although there is still some weakness yet to be resolved (Falchi et al., 2016; Kyba et al., 2013, 2015), or other large scale assessments (Bennie et al., 2015; Cauwels et al., 2014). Undoubtedly, this is possible because of the new advances and development of powerful techniques related to remote sensing, geographical information systems and statistical software. We hypothesise that this research line will be one of the most exploited in the future by the scientific community. However, the negative impacts of light pollution on human and ecosystem health could be relegated to the second level of importance because of the increasing and sudden apparition of other issues such as the COVID-19, which is attracting the majority of efforts and economic supports by institutions and research groups (Pai, 2020; Woolston, 2020).
Economy, urban and rural areas, and land-use change. It is well-known that light pollution is an environmental issue with clear economic implications (Gallaway et al., 2010; Olsen et al., 2014). We


Rodrigo-Comino et al. 383
are experiencing never seen growing light use but a drastic reduction remains an ongoing challenge without strong legislation, funding and guidelines/policies for stakeholders and inhabitants (Feder, 2005; Stanley et al., 2015). To date, research on the social-economic effects of light pollution has been neglected. One notable example would be the publication by Ngarambe et al. (2018) using the Kuznets curve for variable light pollution and land prices. This method shows the interconnected relationship among variables and has been successfully using for other environmental issues (Mosconi et al., 2020). In this study, however, their results did not show an inverted-U relationship between the two above-mentioned variables. A total of 29 articles highlight new engineering solutions aimed to reduce light pollution that focus on LEDs (light-emitting diode) and other lamps of a new generation (e.g. Narendran et al., 2016; Quintana-Barcia et al., 2018; Sun et al., 2017b). It is well known that outdoor streetlights are among the most expensive costs for municipalities, but there is limited research conducted to assess their efficiency. Mockey Coureaux and Manzano (2013) indicated that one of the major causes of blackouts could be the accumulation of dirt on the luminaires (lamps and lighting fixtures), which directly influence depreciation. Light pollution from indoor artificial lighting in office buildings are usually not considered for human wellbeing or large scale assessments (Du et al., 2018). It was estimated that LED could reach 70% of the outdoor and residential lighting markets by 2020, but there is a relevant debate about the impacts on wildlife, animal welfare and disease transmission (Wakefield et al., 2016). In fact, Pawson and Bader (2014) showed that on average traps equipped with LEDs were able to catch 48% more insects than those with HPS (high-pressure sodium vapour lamps), but there was no evidence that colour temperature changes of white LEDs can reduce the ecological impacts. These authors asserted that it is vital to organise collaborative investigations between scientists and engineers to avoid or at least minimise their potential negative impacts. However, some positive results were obtained in relation to human wellbeing and the economy. By estimating the local externalities of investments in LEDs in most populated US counties, Jones (2018) confirmed the significant positive impacts on human well-being, with some variations among respondent demographic types. The most important economic result was related to the per capita monthly household income which amounted to $477 (6.9%). It is interesting to remark that >100 articles were published on the impacts of light pollution within urban, suburban, peri-urban and rural areas and some possible modifications in parallel with land use cover changes. This topic is highly discussed in the recent literature. Hopkins et al. (2018) carefully insisted that light pollution is a ubiquitous feature of urbanisation that change over time and shows an elevated potential to exert strong selection pressures on living organisms, especially, in urban environments. In this regard, Gandy (2017) highlighted that investigation of light pollution mentioning some approaches such as ‘urban atmospheres’, ‘urban ecology’ or ‘smart cities’ are not able to understand the complete difficulty of the politics of light under earlier modernity. This author affirms that the ‘rise of light-emitting diodes (LEDs) or the capitalist transformation of the nocturnal realm’ are usually not well understood. Therefore, we would want to consider new articles that are helping to improve these valid concepts or approaches and its lacks, for example, the modelling research conducted by Ziou and Kerouh (2018) for smart cities, or by Dominoni and Nelson (2018) highlighting the relevance of integrative studies across taxa, biological functions and scientific disciplines from the urban ecology approach. In this line, we strongly consider excellent contributions which can afford: (i) the effects of construction region environments on nighttime brightness considering the regional economic development levels (Wang et al., 2019); (ii) the definition of the city boundaries with dramatic impacts on the scaling dynamic of the night-time light (Operti et al., 2018) and (iii) the combination of technical studies (e.g. using remote sensing data or surveys) and considerations for politicians, decision- and law-makers or governmental agencies (Schuler et al., 2018).


