Conclusions and main remarks
In this review article, we have assessed the literature on the impacts of light pollution on the ecological and human spheres. This is a complement to other reviews on light pollution and updated to 2020. We evaluated >600 scientific documents and confirmed the elevated and diversified nature of research on light pollution considering the broad range of methods used, elements affected and scientific journals published in. However, light pollution is highly affected by external decisions made by stakeholders, ordinary inhabitants, policymakers and politicians from municipalities, regions and countries. These decisions are clearly affecting humans and the natural ecosystem health, but the experts and inhabitants have divergent perspectives, making light pollution a multifaceted term and difficult to be faced, mitigated and not holistically understood. We strongly agree with Clanton (2014) who formulated simple rules to help drastically reduce light pollution, namely: (i) using lights only when necessary; (ii) employing the lowest illuminance as possible and (iii) minimising the light sheds skywards (particularly any close to the horizontal). Also, Gaston et al. (2012) highlighted the necessity to reduce the duration trespass of lighting into territories that are not intended to be lit (including the night sky) and to modify the intensity and spectral composition of lighting. We also add the importance of teaching humankind and promoting engineering research to develop more friendly, aesthetically pleasing and sustainable lighting, taking humans and the natural ecosystems into consideration. This will help to reduce the economic wastes and improve the nighttime, pleasure to observe the sky, nocturnal landscapes and astronomical observations.
